Subject: Light-frame constructors and forwarders, and inline paths for equality, truthiness, elements, `length` and string methods

What this does
  Light-frame constructors and forwarders, and inline paths for equality, truthiness, elements, `length` and string methods.

Why we need it
  `x == null` and `if (obj)` always reached `js_strict_eq_slow` / `js_eq_slow` / `JS_ToBool`. Unary tag tests (`=== null` as a value, `!obj`, `typeof x === "undefined"`) tail-called the out-of-line unary helper. Typed-array reads and every `a[i] = v` took the generic element path.

What it adds
  `regvm_code_light_safe` admits `construct`, and `apply_arguments` in a function that declares no parameters (`regvm_code_has_op`). `regvm_t_construct_light` + `REGVM_FLAG_LIGHT_CTOR`: a light-eligible, non-derived constructor called with `new.target` equal to itself and every declared argument runs in a light frame that owns `this`; `regvm_t_return_light_nested` applies this-unless-object and the unwinder releases `this`. `regvm_t_apply_arguments` enters a light-eligible callee as a light frame; when `apply` is not the builtin, a light forwarder builds the (parameterless, so unmapped-cell) arguments object itself, with `callee` set to its own function and the usual memo slot.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.
